## Why This Is Asked
Gauges understanding of privacy-at-ingest, per-tenant residency, and practical, testable workflows for OTCA telemetry in mobile apps.

## Key Concepts
- Ingest-time policy evaluation
- Per-tenant residency and data partitioning
- Edge gateway to regional streaming
- Deterministic event ordering and testing

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactPayload(category, payload, policy){
  const out = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(payload));
  const cfg = policy[category] || {};
  if (cfg.redact) {
    for (const field of cfg.redact){ delete out[field]; }
  }
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify per-tenant data residency after changes?
- How would you evolve the schema for new diagnostics fields?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests ability to design a schema-evolution friendly path in an OTCA pipeline, a critical, often overlooked area for long-lived telemetry.

## Key Concepts
- Schema Registry
- Versioning and migrations
- Per-tenant isolation
- Data fidelity and compatibility

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo mapping
function mapEvent(event, registry) {
  const latest = registry.latestFor(event.type);
  const v = event.schema_version ?? 1;
  if (v < latest.version) {
    return migrate(event, v, latest.version);
  }
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle breaking changes across tenants?
- How would you test migrations at scale?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical privacy controls in a real OTCA path, focusing on consent-driven data handling and per-tenant residency.

## Key Concepts
- Ingest pipelines with edge gateways and Kafka
- Privacy controls: redact/drop audio data, store digest only
- Per-tenant residency and retention windows

## Code Example
```javascript
function redact(event) {
  if (!event.consent_opt_in) delete event.audio_payload;
  event.audio_digest = hash(event.audio_payload || '');
  delete event.audio_payload;
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test quota enforcement across tenants?
- How would you monitor privacy policy violations in near real time?

## Why This Is Asked
Explores practical RT-level data governance in OTCA: retention, holds, residency, and auditability across regions.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy store; region-scoped purges
- Data tagging: retention_version, tombstones
- Holds vs purge: immutable logs, legal holds
- Storage: object-lock Parquet; Kafka retention
- Testing: synthetic holds, burst scenarios, cross-region audits

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple eligibility check for purge
function eligibleForPurge(record, policy, currentDate) {
  const byTenant = policy.tenant_id === record.tenant_id;
  const byRegion = policy.region === record.region;
  const ageDays = (currentDate - new Date(record.createdAt)) / (1000*60*60*24);
  const notOnHold = !record.hold_id || !policy.hold_ids.includes(record.hold_id);
  return byTenant && byRegion && ageDays > policy.retention_days && notOnHold;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you audit deletion correctness across millions of records?
- How would you handle privacy-rights requests that require immediate data erasure despite holds?
